Title: What They Called Recovery
Credit: Animated feature screenplay — source-developed working scene
Author: Christopher Kokkinos
Draft date: 2026-10-10
Status: Independent screenplay development under DEC-0019; refinements during production

EXT. ROADSIDE PULL-OFF - FRIDAY - MORNING

Voss marks the moving tracker on a paper map. Reyes arrives in the grey estate with coffee; one lid is marked BLACK. Vale checks hers before drinking.

VOSS
You passed the track twice.

REYES
First established the road. Second checked the response.

VOSS
You may have created it.

Vale enlarges Reyes's dash-camera image: a narrow gap in the cottage shutter.

VALE
Someone could have watched. That's a sequence, not proof.

Voss assigns separate routes: Reyes stays local, Vale traces ownership, Voss follows the device. Nobody enters a confirmed site alone.

EXT./INT. VALLEY/RECORDS OFFICE/DEPOT - FRIDAY TO MONDAY

Reyes uses an ordinary builder's van, asks about dogs, buys eggs, gathers uncertain sightings. He waits outside the gate.

Vale joins old title entries to dissolved companies, a dead accountant and a correspondence address. She lays the pages in order.

VALE
An identity bridge. Enough to explain access. Not the man.

VOSS (PHONE)
Mac?

VALE
Not yet. His file begins after this name disappears.

SUNDAY: Vale joins Reyes. Together they inspect the cottage. Washed bowls, disturbed pantry dust, a chair below the shutter. In the barn, the hatchback and fresh tracker adhesive. Beyond the stream, tyre impressions from a different car.

REYES
What kind of driver keeps cars beside forgotten houses?

Vale looks at Mac's photograph rather than answering.

MONDAY: a depot manager watches Voss photograph and recover the tracker from a refrigerated trailer. Knife marks and the reconnected battery show deliberate transfer.

DEPOT MANAGER
Is it police?

VOSS
No.

DEPOT MANAGER
What is it then?

VOSS
A message.

INT. QUARRY CAFE - MONDAY - AFTERNOON

Tracker in an evidence bag. Title history beside it. Voss draws possible exits; none is a known destination.

REYES
He told us one man and a child.

VALE
The girl was probably watching the road.

REYES
She's thirteen. Not trained.

VALE
Mac's paperwork reduced her to cargo.

Voss draws a second circle beside the first.

VOSS
Elias isn't making every decision alone.

REYES
You're giving her too much credit.

VOSS
We gave her none. That was less accurate.

Reyes rubs his thumb against his coffee cup. Vale folds the identity chain. They have a better model, not the farmhouse.
